# React Coding Rules

## Industry Standards

Most React 19+ developers (90%+) follow these practices:

| Practice | This protects you from... |
|----------|---------------------------|
| `Suspense + use()` for data fetching | Race conditions, memory leaks, manual loading state bugs |
| `useOptimistic + startTransition` for instant feedback | Complex rollback logic, janky UX |
| `useTransition` for heavy updates | UI freezing, unresponsive inputs |
| `useSyncExternalStore` for subscriptions | Tearing in concurrent mode, SSR hydration mismatches |
| `<Activity>` for visibility toggle | Lost form state, unnecessary re-mounts |
| `ts-pattern` for 4+ conditions | Non-exhaustive handling, runtime type errors |
| Extract hooks at 3+ useState | God components, untestable logic |

## ❌ Violations

| Legacy Pattern | Consequence |
|----------------|-------------|
| `useState(loading) + useEffect(fetch)` | Race conditions, memory leaks on unmount |
| Manual optimistic + try/catch rollback | Error-prone, inconsistent UI states |
| `{isOpen && <Component />}` | State lost on every toggle |
| `useEffect` for external subscriptions | Tearing bugs, SSR hydration errors |
| if/else chains for discriminated unions | Missed cases, no compile-time safety |
| 5+ useState in one component | Unmaintainable, hard to test |

